WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.575
And the worst one: a collision.

2
00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:05.825
The order matters, so learn it as a list.

3
00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:07.775
First, people.

4
00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:11.000
Check your passengers and crew for injuries.

5
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.975
Second, your boat.

6
00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:15.400
Check for damage and flooding.

7
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.625
Third, the other vessel.

8
00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:26.250
Federal law requires you to render assistance to the people affected, as far as you can without serious danger to your own vessel.

9
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:32.225
Fourth, exchange information: names, addresses, and vessel identification.

10
00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:35.175
Never leave the scene without doing that.

11
00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:38.150
And fifth, report it as required.

12
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.825
For you, that’s the Coast Guard, as a commercial marine casualty.

13
00:00:43.825 --> 00:00:45.750
That’s lesson ten.

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.875
When in doubt, don’t go.

15
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:51.925
Ebb against an onshore wind is the dangerous inlet.

16
00:00:52.175 --> 00:00:54.775
Tow with a bridle and a long line.

17
00:00:55.025 --> 00:00:58.000
Aground: check for leaks before you move.

18
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.950
And after a collision, people first.

19
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.900
Next time: the emergency you practice for and hope never to have, man overboard.
